    feat(harness): harness_resolve.py — one place resolves directive > lane > matrix > hermes, with named refusals. Addresses CF-230.
    
    specs/HARNESS-MATRIX.md §4 is the design authority and nothing implemented it: the node's
    provisioning decided which harness existed, so a directive naming another one just failed. The
    table now lives in data and one resolver applies the order.
    
    - scripts/harness_resolve.py (new, stdlib only): --explain <model> with --lane, --lane-class,
      --directive-harness, --lane-harness, --node-harnesses, --shims, --matrix-file, --json. Model id
      -> family; lane class from the final lane segment (qa|pm|sync|dogfood|releng|docs|readme|perf|
      review = satellite, else foreman). Refusals are named, never silent: harness_directive_unknown,
      harness_lane_unknown, harness_unsupported: <h>; node has: <set>, harness_foreman_requires_hermes
      (spec I1), harness_cannot_host: <harness> <family>, harness_matrix_invalid.
    - config/harness-matrix.json (new): the shipped table — default hermes, claude->claude-code,
      openai->codex, deepseek->dsh, oss->opencode, hosts and shims per §3.
    - tests/test_harness_resolve.py (new, 28 tests): the four rungs with their reasons, the family and
      lane-class tables, every refusal asserted by exit code, the AC3 record keys (model_served
      present and null before a run), a swapped matrix proving the table is data, and a §2-sync case
      that reads specs/HARNESS-MATRIX.md at test time.
    - tests/selftest.sh: one CF-230 row under section 12 — no new numbered section and no census
      change (cf198-section-mapping.sh still prints arms_plan=55 arms_tree=55).
    
